<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rohilla War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1773">1773</a>–<a href="/years/1774">1774</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rohilla War of 1773–1774 was a campaign in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a>, Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, aided by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> troops lent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a>, invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a>. The Rohilla leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafiz-rahmat-khan">Hafiz Rahmat Khan</a> was defeated and killed at Miranpur Katra in April 1774, and most of Rohilkhand was annexed to Awadh. Hastings's role was later attacked at his impeachment.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rohilla-war.jpg" alt="Rohilla War" width="320" height="249" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lord Moira · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rohilla_Horsemen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a> were descendants of Afghan highlanders settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. A few years before the war, driven into the mountains by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a>, they had appealed for help to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a>, Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a>, then an ally of the British. When their chiefs refused to pay what he demanded in return, the Nawab, acting on behalf of the Mughal emperor, resolved to annex their country and asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a> for help, which was given for a sum of forty lakhs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupee">rupees</a>. Hastings argued that the Rohillas endangered the British by uncovering the flank of Awadh.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 23 April 1774 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hafiz-rahmat-khan">Hafiz Rahmat Khan</a> were defeated by Colonel Alexander Champion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> at the Battle of Miranpur Katra, where Hafiz Rahmat Khan was killed. The survivors fled to the hills, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizullah-khan">Faizullah Khan</a> the remaining loyal Rohillas held out in a forest in the hills of Lal Dang, where the British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> forces besieged them from late August into October. Both sides were worn down. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuja-ud-daula">Shuja-ud-Daula</a>, who was suffering from cancer in his leg, wanted a quick peace; he released Mohabbat Khan, a son of Hafiz Rahmat Khan, to weaken Faizullah's authority while keeping in contact with Faizullah himself. On 7 October 1774 Faizullah signed the treaty of Lal Dang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohilkhand">Rohilkhand</a> fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> and was plundered and occupied. Most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rohillas">Rohillas</a> left, crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> to carry on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war or emigrating, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizullah-khan">Faizullah Khan</a> received a state of his choosing under British protection, which became Rampur State. Later the claims of his son Ghulam Muhammad Khan Bahadur, who had seized Rampur from his brother Muhammad Ali Khan, led to the Second Rohilla War in 1794. The campaign of 1773-1774 became a matter of Westminster politics during the impeachment of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-hastings">Warren Hastings</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-burke">Edmund Burke</a>, and later Thomas Macaulay, charged him with destroying a nation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Rohilla War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With Company troops, paid for by Oudh, and Company aid in the First <b>Rohilla War</b>, Oudh gained the whole of Rohilkhand and the Middle Doab in 1774, leaving only Rampur State as a Rohilla enclave.</blockquote><a href="/empire/awadh-state">Awadh State · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1774 the Nawab of the Awadh State, with East India Company troops, defeated and killed Hafiz Rahmat Khan in the <b>Rohilla War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/rohilla-state">Rohilla State</a></li></ul></section>
